===============================
TERMS: Consider memops in program order. So above means earlier and below means latter. Jump
up (or above) means reorder past an earlier instruction.


Good now lets see:

-> loads can jump above stores (respecting RAW)

-> no other memops are allowed to jump past each other for writeback mem

-> lfence allows stores to jump inside and none else

-> sfence allows loads to jump in and out. disallows same for stores.

-> mfence allows no jumping past each other :-)

-> locked instructions dont allow any jumping past each other either
   <hence the adage of using lock:add [esp], 0 for lock acquires>
   <per DaveD on AMD mfence has terribly pipeline latency compared to locked xadd tos,0> 

